WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:20.212
Smoke filled the stable. The other horses were restless and coughing.

2
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:24.503
It was dark, and all the smoke made it impossible to see what was happening.

3
00:00:24.504 --> 00:00:29.908
I coughed again. It was hard to breathe.

4
00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:34.714
The crackling and snapping sound overhead was getting louder.

5
00:00:34.715 --> 00:00:39.088
I didn't know what it was, but it terrified me.

6
00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:42.228
I think it scared the other horses too.

7
00:00:42.229 --> 00:00:47.997
They all started stamping, whinnying, or kicking at their stalls.

8
00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:51.014
I heard rushing footsteps outside.

9
00:00:51.015 --> 00:00:54.540
A moment later the younger stable worker burst in.

10
00:00:54.541 --> 00:00:57.825
The fresh air he brought with him made it easier to breathe.

11
00:00:57.826 --> 00:01:00.710
But I was still very frightened.

12
00:01:00.711 --> 00:01:04.453
The worker grabbed the first horse in the row and tried to lead her out.

13
00:01:04.454 --> 00:01:09.373
But that horse was as scared as I was and refused to go.

14
00:01:09.374 --> 00:01:13.310
After a moment the man gave up and went to the next stall.

15
00:01:13.311 --> 00:01:18.384
But that horse pulled back as well, and so did the next.

16
00:01:18.385 --> 00:01:20.855
Finally the man came to my stall.

17
00:01:20.856 --> 00:01:25.813
He darted in and reached for my halter, but I whirled away.

18
00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:31.300
When he tried again, he managed to grab it. He gave a tug for me to follow.

19
00:01:31.301 --> 00:01:34.709
I refused, planting my feet where they were.

20
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.179
I didn't know him, and I was too frightened to trust a stranger.

21
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.341
I wasn't going anywhere!

22
00:01:41.342 --> 00:01:43.999
Now I realize how foolish that was.

23
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.819
The stable worker was only trying to rescue all the horses.

24
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.776
But at the time we were out of our minds with fear.

25
00:01:52.777 --> 00:02:01.912
That terrible crackling sound was all around, and it seemed safer to stay in my stall than risk getting closer to whatever was causing it.

26
00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:07.224
My stall had always been a safe place, whether at home or on the road.

27
00:02:07.225 --> 00:02:10.507
How was I to know that it was different this time?

28
00:02:10.508 --> 00:02:15.877
Finally the worker gave up. He rushed out of the stable again, shouting for help.

29
00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:19.940
A moment later the old man with the crooked leg came in.

30
00:02:19.941 --> 00:02:23.350
He was much calmer than the other man had been.

31
00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:27.209
He walked up to the first horse and talked to her softly.

32
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:33.654
A second later the mare went with him, though she still looked anxious.

33
00:02:33.655 --> 00:02:38.345
The old man led her outside, then returned and went to the second horse.

34
00:02:38.346 --> 00:02:42.751
At the same time, James hurried in and came to my stall.

35
00:02:42.752 --> 00:02:49.855
"Come, my beauties, it's time to get out of here," he said to Ginger and me in his usual calm, cheerful voice.

36
00:02:49.856 --> 00:02:54.357
I was glad to see him, but I was still feeling very nervous.

37
00:02:54.358 --> 00:03:00.160
James pulled the scarf from around his neck and slipped it over my eyes so I couldn't see anything.

38
00:03:00.161 --> 00:03:07.133
I still heard the fire crackling, but the sound of James talking softly to me was closer and much more familiar.

39
00:03:07.134 --> 00:03:11.030
I decided to trust James and let him tell me what to do.

40
00:03:11.031 --> 00:03:14.696
After all, he had never let me down before.

41
00:03:14.697 --> 00:03:20.806
So I allowed him to lead me out, and a moment later, there was fresh air all around me.

42
00:03:20.807 --> 00:03:23.509
"Somebody hold this horse while I go back in!"

43
00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.936
James called, pulling the scarf off my eyes so I could see again.

44
00:03:27.937 --> 00:03:33.537
A man stepped up to take me, and James ran back into the burning stable.

45
00:03:33.538 --> 00:03:40.218
I was still feeling scared and uncertain, and I didn't want him to go, so I whinnied after him.

46
00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:44.497
Later Ginger told me that my whinny was what had saved her.

47
00:03:44.498 --> 00:03:50.056
Knowing that I was outside, she found the courage to follow James out of her stall.

48
00:03:50.057 --> 00:03:52.731
Through the smoke I watched the doorway.

49
00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:56.674
A moment later James and Ginger appeared in the doorway.

50
00:03:56.675 --> 00:04:04.018
By now the entire roof was blazing. We could hear things collapsing inside.

51
00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:07.570
Someone called out that the fire engine was on its way.

52
00:04:07.571 --> 00:04:10.724
The little old man was bringing other horses out.

53
00:04:10.725 --> 00:04:14.858
But we didn't stay to watch any longer.

54
00:04:14.859 --> 00:04:20.853
The squire had appeared, and he and James led Ginger and me to a different hotel.

55
00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:24.801
In the morning all the humans were talking about what had happened.

56
00:04:24.802 --> 00:04:30.959
The stable workers had gotten most of the other horses out, but two had been lost in the fire.

57
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.343
I was glad that Ginger and I were safe in a different hotel stable, but I felt sad about those two horses.

58
00:04:37.344 --> 00:04:41.579
Everyone was sure that the young man's pipe had started the blaze.

59
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.558
He said he'd put the pipe down before going up into the loft, just as the stable worker had asked.

60
00:04:47.559 --> 00:04:50.576
But nobody believed him.

61
00:04:50.577 --> 00:04:54.949
I was very glad that John had a strict rule at home about such things.

62
00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:59.738
Nobody was allowed to have a pipe or any other fire anywhere near the stable.

63
00:04:59.739 --> 00:05:05.391
I couldn't understand why that wasn't a rule at every stable.

64
00:05:05.392 --> 00:05:11.614
It certainly would have saved those two poor horses.
